            <ArticleTitle>Minimally aggressive treatment of spontaneous coronary artery dissections.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>166-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2459/JCM.0b013e3283529014</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous coronary artery dissection--the separation of the coronary artery wall layers caused by an intramural hematoma--frequently causes an acute coronary syndrome (ACS) and sudden cardiac death. The diagnosis is made at angiography by the 'tram-track' appearance of the contrast media in the true and false lumina, separated by a radiolucent intimal flap. Here we report two cases of spontaneous coronary artery dissection-related ACS managed with minimally aggressive treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
